| 11 | 4 | Attack together. *How does my team move the ball into the other team's area, and how do we decide who does what so everyone is part of it?* | 11 | g4-attack-ideas; g4-team-roles; g4-with-others | SHAPE.S2.5.2; SHAPE.S3.5.1; SHAPE.S3.5.2; SHAPE.S4.5.2; SHAPE.S4.5.5 | Cues: "spread out, pass to open, move to space"; the tactics-question; team talk. Settings: three-on-three keep-away and end-zone games with no elimination, on several small courts | Explain how to attack where teams move into the other team's area: spread out, pass to open players, move to space (S2.5.2); explain how others see a situation, a player who is left out or a slower player (S3.5.1), and talk with others to decide who does what (S3.5.2); describe how favorite activities connect a child with others (S4.5.2) and spot difficulties a group faces when moving together, too little space, uneven skill, one player taking every turn, with a fix (S4.5.5). Calm steps return when a game gets heated (review). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 1's safe-movement tactics and unit 5's kindness in groups. Out: defending (grade 5). Ends: 2 check days (skill task: moves to open space and receives a pass in a three-on-three game, unit 10's pass cue watched at the unit's first skill day and again here; knowledge tasks: names two attacking moves in a game picture; one connection and one group difficulty with a fix; conduct: takes a role and decides with the group using team talk, S3.5.2), 1 respond day | After unit 10 because attacking is passing to open players; team roles and group difficulties are taught here because small-sided team play is where they first arise; it sits before the mid-year check so the check samples standards 3 and 4 | Candidate: a group plan card where the team writes who does what before a game | Teams made privately by the teacher; a more and a less competitive court offered; no scores posted. Pinnies reused. Cost: $0 |
