# Glossary: the locked words

These are the locked words: the best known word for each thing we build, the same word inside and outside the
project (settled 2026-11-12, card 112 `q-course-level-names`, see DESK-ANSWERS "course level names"; the naming scan
is docs/reviews/naming-scan-2026-11-12.md). One row per thing we build. New prose, page copy, script messages and
Desk cards use the word in the first column. A code name (file, URL, column, id) may lag behind its word; where it
does, the row says so and why, and the staged plan in the naming scan says when it moves.

Two rules sit above the tables:

- **"Curio Class" in full**, always. `Curio.*` (the kit's code namespace, `kit/curio.js`) is code, not a name.
- **The build steps keep their names:** Idea, Spec, Build, Self-check, Refine, Fix, Exit check, Art and voice,
  Finish check, Sign-off, Live (docs/agents/decisions/BASELINE.md). Because "Exit check" is a step, nothing a
  student sees is called an exit check.

The mathematical senses of unit (unit fraction, square unit, units of measure) are not course words; they keep
their ordinary meaning.

## Older words, and where they stay

Code, ids and dated records still carry older words. Read them this way:

| Older word | Means today |
|---|---|
| stretch | unit |
| unit (the 1 to 6 day package) | section |
| stretch sheet | unit plan |
| unit plan (the old section-level plan) | section plan |
| spine, year spine | scope and sequence |
| end check | end-of-unit assessment |
| check day | assessment day |
| exit slip | exit ticket |
| Use (a day kind) | application |
| module (a topic page) | topic |
| lesson (a tool role) | teach |

**Where older names stay on purpose:** ids, item ids, folder and file names keep their old letters and words:
`s11-...` item ids, plan files `sNN.md`, section folders `uNN-N/`, `math-4-spine.md` and the
other `-spine.md` files, docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md (the section and unit plan template) and
docs/courses/LESSON-DAY.md, `module_id` and `data/modules.csv`, the worksheet lists' "Module" column. Each row
below names its lag.

**Records keep their words.** Dated reports in docs/reviews and docs/archive, and DESK-ANSWERS entries before its
2026-11-12 "course level names" entry, keep the words of their day: read them with the table above ("unit" there is
today's section, "stretch" is today's unit, "spine" is scope and sequence).

## The course

| Word | What it is | Do not call it | Code name and URL, if different |
|---|---|---|---|
| **course** | One grade of one subject (grade 4 math) or one high school course. What a teacher runs from day 1 to the last | class (worksheets), program, track | The frame slug (`math-grade-4`, `biology`): `docs/courses/<course>/`, `data/plans/<course>/`, `/courses/<course>/`; `data/plans/frames.csv` maps it to its catalogue id, subject and grade. Lag: grade 4 math's plan folder is still `math-4` (`legacy_plan_id`); `data/courses.csv` `course_id` (`math-k8`), the `catalog_id` column, is a subject track across grades, not a course; the worksheet code calls a course-sized list a `unit` |
| **scope and sequence** | Every day of a course's year in order, with the reason for the order, the standards and the pacing (PRODUCTION-PATH step 3, brief docs/pipeline/briefs/sequence-write.md) | spine, year spine, pacing guide (a pacing guide is the calendar only), map | `docs/courses/<course>/scope-and-sequence.md`, day rows in `data/plans/<course>/days.csv`. Lag: older files `docs/courses/<course>-spine.md` |
| **unit** | A run of course days (5 to 42 across the courses; grade 4 math 8 to 22) on one block of standards. A readiness check, given before it opens, precedes it where it assumes an earlier grade row last used two or more grades back; an end-of-unit assessment inside its days checks what it taught, and planned response follows; a unit over 25 days also holds a mid-unit assessment inside its days. The frame and the scope and sequence set its length | stretch, module, topic, chapter | Lag for good: item ids `s11-...` and plan files `sNN.md` keep the `s`; ids are never reused (LESSON-DAY rule 8); `days.csv` `stretch` |
| **section** | A short package of days (1 to 6) inside a unit, with its own plan and opener (section 11.1 is the first section of unit 11) | unit, part (taken by "part of a standard"), topic, lesson set | Lag: folders `courses/<course>/uNN-N/`, `data/units.csv` and its `unit_id` |
| **end-of-unit assessment**, short **unit assessment**; **assessment day** | The assessment near the end of a unit, inside its days, on every part of every standard the unit teaches. It is followed by the response time the plan names, in the same day or on later respond days, counted once. In K to 2 an observation task spread over the unit's last lessons may stand for it | end check, check day, unit test, quiz | `items.csv` `check` = `end`; `days.csv` `kind` = `Check`, `assess` = `end` (scope stage) |
| **mid-unit assessment** | An assessment partway through a unit over 25 days, inside its days (the base texts' own: ELA K to 5, math 6 to 8) | mid-unit check | `days.csv` `assess` = `mid` |
| **course-level check** (mid-year, year-end as adjectives) | An assessment outside every unit that covers several units, set in the frame's Course-level checks table and followed by its respond days | course check, cumulative check, year check, benchmark, year review (review days are their own row) | `days.csv` `stretch` = `C`, `unit` blank, `assess` = `course` |
| **day**, **course day** | One numbered day of a course's year (day 90 of 160), of any kind: teaching, assessment, respond or review. A frame's day totals are course days | lesson day, period, teaching day (that is one kind) | `d090.md`; `/courses/<course>/day-<n>/`; `days.csv` `day` |
| **school day**, **week** | A position in the 160-day, 32-week school year that every course shares (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md): week 1 is school days 1 to 5. A course meeting every day has course day n on school day n; one meeting fewer days spreads its course days over its weeks | date (a school sets its own dates) | `data/plans/calendar.csv`; `days.csv` `school_day` |
| **lesson** | What one teaching day ships: teacher page, student page, exit ticket, answer key, display | lesson day | The template is docs/courses/LESSON-DAY.md (file name lags) |
| **teaching day, assessment day, respond day** | The kinds of day. A teaching day is understanding, fluency or application, in every subject (the goal says the activity: an inquiry, a lab, a discussion, a draft); a respond day acts on an assessment. The kind is what the day does, and this row is the one home of the definitions: **Understanding** is the first explicit teaching of anything new (a skill, concept, method, model, term set, text structure, or a standard's first day in the course), a bridge that teaches ahead of another subject included; **Fluency** is practice of what earlier days taught, so a Fluency day teaches nothing new and drafts, revises and edits no piece of writing; **Application** uses taught work on a new task or case (an inquiry, a lab, a design, a performance), and every day that drafts, revises or edits a piece of writing is Application. Where a day both first-teaches and drafts, first teaching wins (Understanding). Work made on a Fluency day may still be collected and scored later after feedback and revision (unit-lens.md). sequence.py fails the plainest mislabels (settled 2026-11-17, see DESK-ANSWERS) | check day | `days.csv` `kind`: `Understanding`, `Fluency`, `Application`, `Check` (an assessment day), `Respond` |
| **warm-up** | The first minutes of a day (a grammar warm-up in ELA) | opener (that is the section's teacher page), opening, grammar opener | `days.csv` `opens`, `opens_min` |
| **goal** (on screen and paper: the "I can" line) | What the day is for, in student words | objective, target | `days.csv` `goal` |
| **section opener** | The teacher page for a section: the content at an adult's level, why this model, the common errors, the supply list | unit opener, unit overview, intro | `opener.md` in each section folder |
| **readiness check** | The short set of items, given before a unit opens so its results shape day 1, on what the unit builds on from an earlier grade row last used two or more grades back (a row used the year before, or earlier the same year, gets a retrieval task instead; sequence-write.md rule 2). It takes the last ten minutes of the previous unit's last day, or of the respond day between the two units; unit 1's takes the last ten minutes of day 1, after the hook; never on any other unit's first day, in every course (settled 2026-11-14, see DESK-ANSWERS) | pre-test, diagnostic, pre-assessment | `items.csv` `check` = `readiness`; `readiness.md`; `days.csv` `assess` = `readiness` (on the day it is given) |
| **readiness day** | The day whose last ten minutes hold the next unit's readiness check | | |
| **retrieval task** | A short look back inside a day at something taught before; not a check and not marked | readiness check, opening retrieval task | |
| **facts check** | The multiplication facts check of grade 4 math, given in days 1 to 6 and before day 94 | fluency test, timed test | `facts-check-N.md` |
| **sub-ready day** | One day per unit in each course (9 a year in grade 4 math), and in ELA K to 5 one per base unit (three per Curio Class unit), written with the unit, that a substitute can run from its page (settled 2026-11-12, see DESK-ANSWERS) | Sub Day (that is the per-tool packet), sub plan | Not built |
| **at home** block | The one-child path every day carries for a parent teaching at home; built as the day page's At home view | homeschool version, home track | `### At home` in the day file; `<day>-home.html` |
| **Teaching map** | The picture of every course's order. Hidden now; it comes back, reimagined, after the courses are built (settled 2026-11-12, see DESK-ANSWERS) | The Map, teaching map (lower case t in a name) | `/map/`, build flag `--map`, `data/sequence.csv` |
| **progression** | The layer above the course: one per subject, all its grades, saying what each grade owns, assumes from the grade before and hands on (ROADMAP layer 4) | learning progression (that is the research term), vertical alignment, map | `data/progressions/<subject>.csv`; the page `docs/courses/math-3-5-progression.md` |
| **course frame** | The course-level summary: grades, the standards and progression skills it owns, its day total, its unit list with a reason for each, and its source map where it has a base text. Its scope and sequence then sets every day | course map, course outline, spine | `docs/courses/<course>/frame.md`, totals and catalogue id in `data/plans/frames.csv` (`scripts/frame.py sync`); PRODUCTION-PATH step 2 |
| **check set** | The sources a subject checks its units against beyond the anchor standard (social studies: California, Tennessee, New York and the college-level frameworks; math: the Progressions; science: the practice and crosscutting elements; ELA: candidate texts). Not a student assessment | | the frame's "Check set adds" column; a cell starts "Candidate:" until the scope and sequence settles it |
| **lean** | A progression row's use of another subject's skill, as a prerequisite or a context link | dependency, cross-link | `data/progressions/<subject>.csv` `cross_subject`; the frame's Cross-subject section |
| **bridge** | The teaching a unit does itself when the other subject has not yet taught a lean (the frame's "if later" line); its time is inside the unit's days | local bridge, workaround | |
| **source map** | One row per base-text lesson, placed in one of our units and days with a keep, split, merge, cut or ours decision | course map, crosswalk, alignment | `data/plans/<course>/source-map.csv` |
| **baseline** | A whole-structure review at a set point in the build order, after which the work is the new reference (baseline 1: all progressions and frames; baseline 2: social studies down to its units) | milestone, checkpoint | PRODUCTION-PATH section 3 |
| **probe**, **guard** | A few days written from finished unit plans to test them, with "on" and "off" set beforehand. The probe runs as each subject's units are done; the guard runs in every subject before full writing | pilot (a pilot was a unit built to the bottom first; no longer part of the plan) | PRODUCTION-PATH P4, steps 4 and 7 |

## Inside a lesson

| Word | What it is | Do not call it | Code name, if different |
|---|---|---|---|
| **teacher page** | The page the teacher reads the night before; it runs the day | lesson plan (that is the per-tool page), script | `<day>-teacher.html` |
| **student page** | The one sheet each student works on, both sides at most | worksheet, handout, student sheet | `<day>-student.html` |
| **exit ticket** | The quarter-page check at the end of a teaching day, four to a sheet; one question on it is an **exit question** | exit slip, exit check (a build step), cool-down | `items.csv` `check` = `exit`; `days.csv` `exit` |
| **answer key** (short **key** in code and file names only) | Every answer, its working and what each wrong answer means | key (in prose: it clashes with key tool and `judged` = `key`) | `<day>-key.html` |
| **display** | The projector pages, generated in the order of the teacher page | slides, deck, presentation | `<day>-display.html` |
| **slides** | None. We do not make them | | |
| **record sheet** | The class list where a teacher records each exit ticket and assessment, one per unit (in ELA K to 5 one per base unit); also the portfolio at home | gradebook, tracker | `record.md` |
| **family note** | The short note home, one per unit (in ELA K to 5 one per base unit), English and Spanish | parent letter, newsletter | `family.md` |
| **common errors** (inside the project: **error register**) | The lettered wrong ways of thinking a unit expects, and what to do about each | misconceptions list | `errors.csv`, `wrongs.csv` |
| **materials** | What to print and gather, per student and per class | supplies list (fine in prose) | the `materials` header line |
| **worked example**, **fix-it**, **practice**, **challenge** | The kinds of question inside a lesson | | `items.csv` `check` = `example`, `fix`, `practice`, `challenge` |
| **word** (a highlighted word; the box is **Words today**) | A word a day introduces, highlighted on the page with its student definition and fun fact, defined once per sense and grade band (Desk q-word-names-1, 2026-11-25). Inside the ledger the same thing is a **term**: what a day introduces, with the teacher-facing meaning | vocabulary word, key term, glossary entry | `data/plans/vocabulary.csv` (the word table; not `words.csv`, which in twelve courses holds banned patterns); mark `[[word:<sense>]]` or `[[word:<sense>|shown]]`; a **sense id** is the word plus a meaning tag, `cell-biology`; ledger `kind` = `term` |

## The site: four doors

| Word | What it is | Do not call it | URL and code name, if different |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Courses** | The door for teaching a course day by day | Curriculum, Lessons | `/courses/` (not built) |
| **Find** | The door for something for tomorrow: search, standards, topics, grades, subjects | Browse, Classrooms | Not built. Today's `/classrooms/` page, its **grade rooms** and **subject wings**, fold in here and those three names retire |
| **Shelves** | The door for everything usable on its own: tools, the Supply Closet, the Reading Room, collections | Library (that is the Reading Room's code name), catalog | Not built. One named collection on it is **a shelf** (the Word Wall shelf) |
| **Explore** | The door for curiosity and play: Curiosities, Recess, play styles | Fun, Games | Not built |
| **topic** (topic page) | One subject-and-grade topic with its tools, worksheets, Word Wall, anchor chart and more, tied to its standards | module | URL `/topics/<id>/`; the old `/modules/<id>/` URLs (1,634 pages) redirect there. Lag: `module_id` in data and scripts, `data/modules.csv`, the search index's `m` field, the "Module" column of the worksheet lists |
| **standard** | One code of a standards document (4.MD.A.3); **part of a standard** is one verb's worth of it | objective, outcome | `/standards/<code>/`; `parts.csv` |
| **strand** | The grouping above a standard that a topic page shows: a domain in math and science, a strand in ELA, a discipline in the arts | domain (fine when quoting a math document) | `modules.csv` `strand` |
| **grade band** | K-2, 3-5, 6-8, 9-12 | span, level | `bands.csv`, `band` |
| **framework** | A standards document (CCSS-M, NGSS) | | `frameworks` |

## The shelves

| Word | What it is | Do not call it | URL and code name, if different |
|---|---|---|---|
| **tool** | One interactive page that works offline as a single file | app, game (unless it is one), activity, interactive, module, instrument | `/tools/<id>/` |
| **key tool**, **light tool** | A section plan's mark: the tool a day is planned around, or a lighter one built from templates | practice tool (the old name of light tool; "practice" is a role) | `days.csv` `mark` |
| **role**: teach, see why, practice | What a tool does (BASELINE rule 21) | lesson (that is what a teaching day ships) | `coverage.csv` `teach`, `see_why`, `practice` |
| **lesson plan** | The one-period plan for one tool, on its tool page | teacher page (that is the course's) | `/tools/<id>/plan/`; its last part is headed "Exit ticket" |
| **Sub Day packet** | The five-page print packet for one tool, for a substitute, on the Supply Closet shelf | sub day (lower case), sub plan, sub packet | `/tools/<id>/sub/`, `/closet/sub-day/`, `exit.md` |
| **on the bench** | Planned, not built yet | coming soon, draft | `bench.html` |
| **Ready to use** | The page of every built tool | | `/tools/` |
| **collection** | Tools of one kind across grades | family (that word is for parents) | Lag: `/collections/<family>/`, `family` column |
| **play style** | How a tool feels to use (arcade, showdown) | format | Lag: `/styles/`, `format`, `data/formats.csv` |
| **Supply Closet** | The print shelves that go with the tools | closet (fine in code) | `/closet/` |
| **printables** | Anything made to print; also the name of the Printables shelf (templates and paper) | | `/closet/printables/` |
| **worksheet** | A printed page of practice questions with its answer key, listed by class on the Worksheets shelf | sheet (fine in prose once named), student page | `/closet/worksheets/`; a class's list is a `unit` in the worksheet code (lag) |
| **worksheet generator** (on screen: "Make a sheet") | Makes a new worksheet and key each time | maker, builder | `/closet/worksheets/make/`, `site/assets/gen/` |
| **Word Wall**, **anchor chart**, **poster**, **Research Card**, **Game Night** (Family Game Night), **Break Box**, **Teacher Desk** (its tool: the **Desk Dock**) | The named shelves | word walls, game night (lower case), the Desk (that is the Review Desk) | `/closet/...` |
| **Reading Room** | The texts to read on screen or in print | library (on screen) | Lag: `/library/`, `library` in code |
| **Curiosities** | Wonder pieces for students, outside the bar | Instruments (the old name) | |
| **Recess** | Play, outside the bar | | `/recess/` |
| **score code** | The code a student shows to report a tool result | score, grade | |

