| 1 | 3 | Warm up, move safe. *How do I get my body ready, stay safe when the game keeps changing, and stay safe without being reminded?* | 11 | g3-warm-cool; g3-safe-movement; g3-stay-safe-own | SHAPE.S2.5.11; SHAPE.S2.5.1; SHAPE.S3.5.4 | Cues: "walk, swing, then stretch slow"; "eyes up, find open space, change direction". Settings: a whole-class warm-up routine, then small dodge and tag games with no one out in marked areas | Tell when a warm-up and a cool-down are needed for different activities (a run, a throwing day, a dance) and lead one of each: a walk and arm swings, then slow stretches only after the warm-up (S2.5.11; first in grade 3, from the tool); use movement ideas and tactics to stay safe where the setting keeps changing: scan, eyes up, choose open space, change direction (S2.5.1; the tactics-question returns as "where is the space, and what is my safe move?"); stay safe on one's own with few reminders: check the space, use equipment correctly, stop on the signal unasked (S3.5.4; near water, stay with a grown-up and wear a life jacket on a boat, one line). Opens with retrieval tasks on K to 2 grade 2 units 15 (why we stretch), 19 (rules keep us safe), 20 (chase and flee tactics) and 21 (movement ideas), in the instant activities of the first skill days. Out: dynamic and static stretch types by name (grade 6). Ends: 2 check days (skill task: a controlled change of direction into open space in a tag game; knowledge task: says when a warm-up is needed for a pictured activity and picks a fitting one; conduct: stops on the signal and checks the space with no reminder, the reminders a private teacher note), 1 respond day. Tools: warm-up and cool-down picture cards, tag game cards (ours) | First because every later lesson opens with the warm-up routine and closes with the cool-down this unit teaches, and every game in the band needs the safe-movement tactics and the stay-safe habits; it reopens the year with grade 2's chase, rules and stretch rows by retrieval | Candidate: students design a three-move warm-up card a partner leads | Static stretches only after the warm-up, no bouncing, to a gentle pull. Tag with no elimination: a light touch on the shoulder or back, a tagged child does a jump task and rejoins. Collision control: marked areas, one direction at first. Water break and shade on warm outdoor days. Cost: $35 (30 pinnies, reused all band) |
| 2 | 3 | Throw, roll, catch. *What are the key parts of a good throw, and how do I use them to hit a target and catch what comes back?* | 11 | g3-throw-roll-catch; g3-key-parts | SHAPE.S1.5.9; SHAPE.S1.5.10; SHAPE.S1.5.12; SHAPE.S2.5.15 | Cues: "side to the target, step with the opposite foot, follow through"; "hands out, eyes on it, soft hands". Settings: to a wall, a hoop and a partner; catches at low, middle and high | Roll a ball from a fixed spot to a target (S1.5.9); throw overhand and underhand in several tasks, to a wall, a hoop and a partner at set distances (S1.5.10); catch at several heights, from a toss and a bounce (S1.5.12); name the key parts of a skill done correctly and find one in a partner's try (S2.5.15; cue-words retrieved and used as a checklist). Begun toward SHAPE.S1.5.17 (owned in grade 4, unit 10): a toss to a partner who has taken a step. Opens with a retrieval task on K to 2 grade 2 units 16 (overhand throw) and 17 (ball stations). Out: passing to a moving partner (grade 4); why a cue works (grade 4, unit 10). Ends: 2 check days (skill task: three of five throws to a target at a set distance with the cues, and three of five catches; knowledge task: names the three parts of the throw and marks one part in a partner's try on a picture card; conduct: one throwing direction kept and balls fetched on the signal, S3.5.4 review), 1 respond day. Tools: soft balls and beanbags one a pair, hoops or chalk targets; Curio Class writes the key-parts cards | Throwing and catching are the base of every later ball unit (5, 6, 10, 12, 18, 21); key parts come with it because the throw is the skill the class knows best from grade 2, so its parts can be named before new skills use the same checklist | Candidate: a partner checklist where each child marks one key part of the other's throw | One throwing direction; soft balls; no human targets; partners chosen by the teacher. Safety read (flying objects). Cost: $30 (6 hoops; reuses K to 2's balls and beanbags) |
| 3 | 3 | Jump, land, balance, roll. *How do I land softly, hold a balance on different body parts, and roll safely on a mat?* | 10 | g3-jump-land-balance; g3-body-roll | SHAPE.S1.5.7; SHAPE.S1.5.8; SHAPE.S1.5.3 | Cues: "bend, swing, land soft, hold"; "tuck chin, round back, hands on the mat". Settings: in place on stable surfaces, then mats; a low partner balance | Jump and land in place: two feet, one foot, a half turn (S1.5.7); balance on different body parts, including a low partner balance, for three counts (S1.5.8); body rolls in a fixed spot on a mat: log roll, egg roll, and a forward roll down a soft incline for children who are ready (S1.5.3). Opens with a retrieval task on K to 2 grade 2 unit 15's travel, jump and balance sequences. Out: traveling chains and feet-to-hands shifts (grade 4). Ends: 1 check day (skill task: three soft landings and two three-count balances, with one roll against two cues as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: says which body parts form the base in a pictured balance; conduct: carries and places mats with a partner and waits for a clear mat, S3.5.4 review), 1 respond day. The last ten minutes of day 32 hold the readiness check for unit 4 | Before the fitness unit so its circuits can use balances and landings; balance and landing in place come before the grade 4 chains and sequences that join them (STANDARD.md 3 rule 3) | Candidate: a balance a partner copies from a picture card the child draws | Mats for all rolls (Required access); no headstands or handstands; no rolling for a child with a neck or back concern from the family or nurse, a log roll or a standing balance task instead; partner balances stay low and the base is chosen by the pair. Safety read (mats). Cost: $0 new (mats from stock) |
| 4 | 3 | Why we move. *Which activities build my heart, muscles and flexibility, and how does moving help my health and let me show who I am?* | 10 | g3-health-fitness; g3-benefits; g3-express-benefits | SHAPE.S2.5.7; SHAPE.S2.5.13; SHAPE.S4.5.1; SHAPE.S4.5.3 | Cues: "hand on heart, count, choose a face"; the fitness-parts chart. Settings: fitness circuits of the year's skills, a favorite-activity station | Describe with examples activities that build the health-related fitness parts: heart and lungs endurance, muscle strength, muscle endurance, flexibility (S2.5.7; fitness-parts introduced as a picture chart; body composition is not taught as a body measure); describe the benefits of being active: a stronger heart and bones, better sleep, a better mood, more energy (S2.5.13); describe how a favorite activity lets a child express themselves (S4.5.1) and explain the health benefits of moving (S4.5.3, in full: the heart, bones and mood); the goal cycle returns as a favorite station picked and tried again. Readiness check on g1-heart-muscle (K to 2 unit 12, grade 1: the heart is a muscle) on day 32; pulse-check and the effort faces open as a retrieval task on unit 1. Ends: 1 check day (skill task: a three-station circuit with soft landings from unit 3, its cue watched at the unit's first skill day and again here; knowledge tasks: matches four activities to four fitness parts; names two benefits; one sentence on how a favorite activity lets them express themselves and one on a health benefit; conduct: encourages a circuit partner, S3.5.3 begun in words), 1 respond day. Tools: fitness-parts chart and activity cards (ours) | After units 1 to 3 because the circuits use their warm-up, landings and throws; the fitness parts are named here, before the mid-year check, so grade 4's skill-related parts (unit 9) add to a chart the class already knows; the two benefits rows and the expression row sit together because S2.5.13 and S4.5.3 describe the same benefits from two sides | Candidate: a private "how I like to move" card for the family note | Bodies: no weighing, no body measurements, no body talk; pulse counts private and never compared; a child with asthma or a heart condition follows the nurse's guidance; a child who prefers not to run shows effort another way. Sensitivity read sources: the federal physical activity guidelines' children's page, the national pediatrics association's pages on school-age activity. Cost: $0 |
| 5 | 3 | Feet and hands on the move. *How do I keep a ball close while I move it with my feet or my hand, and how do I make sure everyone in my group gets a turn?* | 9 | g3-foot-skills; g3-hand-dribble; g3-kind-groups | SHAPE.S1.5.18; SHAPE.S1.5.20; SHAPE.S1.5.19; SHAPE.S3.5.3 | Cues: "plant foot beside the ball, laces through"; "small taps, ball close"; "push, fingers wide, eyes up". Settings: a wall or goal, open space with cones, small groups of four passing to everyone | Kick with the laces to a target and to a partner (S1.5.18); dribble with the feet in open space, changing direction (S1.5.20); bounce a ball with the hands in place and on the move, with one hand and then the other (S1.5.19, in full: in place and on the move); act kindly so a group activity stays positive: invite someone in, pass to everyone, encouraging words, team talk when stuck (S3.5.3; team-talk retrieved from K to 2 unit 17). Opens with a retrieval task on K to 2 grade 2 unit 17's station ball skills. Out: dribbling against defenders (grade 5). Ends: 1 check day (skill task: dribbles through five cones with the feet without bumping, with ten hand-dribbled steps as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: names where the plant foot goes and why the eyes stay up; conduct: passes to everyone in the group and uses an encouraging word, S3.5.3), 1 respond day | After the mid-year check, with throw and catch secure; foot and hand dribbling share the "ball close, eyes up" control, and group passing tasks are where kindness in groups first matters this year | Candidate: a group card where the group checks that everyone touched the ball | Kicks aimed at a wall or goal, never at a person; foam or soft balls for kicking indoors. Groups made privately by the teacher. Cost: $75 (15 foam kicking balls and 15 dribble balls, reused in grades 4 and 5) |
| 6 | 3 | Strike with tools. *How do I hit a ball with a bat or a paddle, and which calming method works best for me when I miss?* | 8 | g3-strike-tools; g3-calm | SHAPE.S1.5.11; SHAPE.S1.5.16; SHAPE.S4.5.9 | Cues: "side to the target, eyes on the ball, swing through"; SEL's four calm-down steps "stop, breathe, name it, choose". Settings: off a tee, a self-toss, a gentle partner toss; one batter per space | Hit with a long-handled tool (plastic bat, hockey-style stick) in several tasks (S1.5.11) and a short-handled tool (paddle, racket) in several tasks (S1.5.16), off a tee, a self-toss and a gentle partner toss; name which calming methods work best during activity (a slow breath, counting, a drink of water, stepping out for a minute with a role) and use one after a miss (S4.5.9). The calm-down steps are SEL's for grades 3 to 5 (g3-sm-calm-down), in SEL's words; the unit opens them as a retrieval task and teaches choosing and using one while moving, on PE's calm-steps card. Opens also with a retrieval task on K to 2 grade 2 unit 19's long-tool striking. Ends: 1 check day (skill task: contacts a gently tossed ball three of five times with a swing through, with three paddle hits to a wall as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: names the calming method that works best for them and shows it; conduct: swing space and the batter's line kept, S3.5.4 review), 1 respond day | Striking a tossed ball needs the tracking of unit 2's catches; it sits before the year-end check so the check samples standard 4; striking misses often, which makes the calming method real | Candidate: a private calm card where each child circles their best method | Swing space cleared; one batter per space; the line stands behind a safe distance; foam balls. Emotions: calming is offered, never a punishment; a child may step out for a reset with a role; the teacher page names the school counselor. Sensitivity read sources: the national child mental health center's pages on children's self-calming. Safety read (striking tools). Cost: $60 (15 short rackets; reuses K to 2's paddles, bats and foam balls) |
| 7 | 3 | Rope and moving outside class. *How many ways can I jump a rope, and how can I be active outside PE this week?* | 7 | g3-rope; g3-active-outside | SHAPE.S1.5.6; SHAPE.S4.5.8 | Cues: "small jumps, turn from the wrists, steady beat". Settings: self-turned forward and backward, a step and a jump, a long rope with turners, a partner rope | Jump rope through several tasks: self-turned forward and backward, a step and a jump, a long rope with a turner, a partner rope (S1.5.6), ten in a row at the child's own pace, growth against their own start; find ways to be active outside PE class (recess games, walking, family play, a park, a home activity with nothing to buy) and pick one to try for a week with the goal cycle (S4.5.8). Opens with a retrieval task on K to 2 grade 2 unit 18's self-turned rope and unit 16's goal cycle. Ends: 1 check day (skill task: ten jumps in a row in one task of the child's choice, against their own first count; knowledge task: names one way to be active outside class and when it will happen, and says after the week how it went; conduct: ropes kept in the rope space and coiled back, S3.5.4 review), 1 respond day | After the year-end check (week 26) because grade 3 weeks 28, 30, 31 and 32 hold core checks; rope is the year's most portable skill, so the week-long try outside class can use it, and summer is near | Candidate: a recess rope rhyme the class makes up | Ropes matched to height; adapted ropes (a rope on the floor to jump side to side) for a range of ability; soft floor. The outside activity names nothing to buy and no place a family must travel to. Cost: $20 (2 long ropes; reuses K to 2's short ropes) |
| 8 | 3 | Dance steps. *How do steps become a phrase, and how do two dances from different places use the beat?* | 8 | g3-dance-steps | | Cues: "listen, count to four, step on the beat". Settings: a longways line with a partner, a circle | Dance steps strung into short four-count phrases in two dance styles, in a line, a circle and with a partner: begun toward SHAPE.S1.5.1 (owned in grade 4, unit 13) and SHAPE.S1.5.5 (owned in grade 4, unit 15). Picks: the Virginia Reel, a longways dance done in the United States since the 1800s from English and Irish country dance roots (the line-dance style), and Alunelul, a Romanian children's song and stamping dance done in a line or circle (the folk style), neither used in K to 2. Opens with a retrieval task on K to 2 grade 2 unit 21's three-move sequence. Ends: 1 check day (skill task: a four-count pattern to the beat in each dance; knowledge task: says one way the two dances differ in formation or step; conduct: kind to a partner in the line, S3.5.3 review), 1 respond day. Tools: music track or the teacher's drum; Curio Class writes the dance cards and each dance's short source line | Last in grade 3 so its phrases hand straight to grade 4's chains and dance sequences; dance returns every year of the band (units 8, 15, 22), never taught once and dropped; after the year-end check because the core checks hold weeks 28 to 32 | Candidate: partners teach each other one phrase | Cultural content: US country dance and Romanian folk traditions; partners set by the teacher with no gender roles (the Virginia Reel is often taught in lines split by gender: here it is not); no costume or imitation of a people. Communities: US traditional dance and Romanian. Spot-checked 2026-11-25: the Virginia Reel in an encyclopedia entry and a Library of Congress record of an 1800s dance manual that covers it (loc.gov/item/afcreed000072, catalog record); Alunelul at a folk dance notes archive (folkdancefootnotes.org, "Alunelu(l), children's song and dance, Romania"); the agent studies a national folklife archive's entry and a Romanian cultural institution's page first. Swap allowed (Swaps). Cost: $0 |
| 9 | 4 | Pulse, agility and the fitness chart. *What does my pulse tell me about how hard I am working, and which activities build speed, balance and quick reactions?* | 10 | g4-pulse-body; g4-skill-fitness | SHAPE.S2.5.12; SHAPE.S2.5.14; SHAPE.S2.5.10 | Cues: "two fingers, wrist or neck, count to ten"; the fitness-parts chart. Settings: rest, warm-up and circuit stations of skill-fitness tasks | Find the pulse at the wrist or neck, count it for ten seconds at rest and after activity, and name activities that raise the heart rate (S2.5.12); notice and describe how activity changes the body: breath, warmth, sweat, a faster beat (S2.5.14); describe and show activities that build the skill-related fitness parts: agility, balance, coordination, power, reaction time and speed, added to the fitness-parts chart (S2.5.10). The heart pumps blood to the working muscles, said in one line (science grade 4 teaches the body's structures later this year, Cross-subject). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 4's fitness parts and benefits and unit 1's warm-up. Out: beats per minute and target zones (grade 6). Ends: 1 check day (skill task: shows one activity for a named skill-related part at a station, with a warm-up led for a partner as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: finds a pulse and says what changed after the circuit; matches an activity to a skill-related part; conduct: waits for a clear station and helps a partner count, S3.5.3 review), 1 respond day. Tools: fitness-parts chart (unit 4's, extended), station cards (ours) | First in grade 4 because every later grade 4 warm-up uses the pulse and every game unit names the skill-related parts it builds; it reopens the year with grade 3's fitness chart through retrieval | Candidate: a private before-and-after pulse card the child keeps | Pulse counts private and never compared or posted; a child who cannot find a pulse uses a hand on the chest; no fitness scores. Sensitivity read sources: the national heart institute's page on the heart and physical activity. Cost: $0 (cones, ropes and balls reused) |
| 10 | 4 | Pass, gather, move and send. *How do I pass to a partner who is moving, and why does each cue make my throw or kick work?* | 11 | g4-pass-gather; g4-move-and-send; g4-object-ideas | SHAPE.S1.5.17; SHAPE.S1.5.21; SHAPE.S2.5.4 | Cues: "step to the partner, aim at the chest, soft hands"; "eyes up, move then send". Settings: standing pairs, then pairs moving a few steps; dribble then send to a goal, hoop or wall | Pass with hands or feet to a partner and gather it, standing and then moving a few steps (S1.5.17, owned here; passing with the hands while moving starts in grade 4, from the tool); combine handling an object with moving to send it at a target: dribble then shoot, catch then throw, foot dribble then pass (S1.5.21); understand which movement ideas make object skills work: step with the opposite foot, follow through, aim, a bent-knee stable base, and say why a cue helps (S2.5.4). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 2's throw and catch and key parts and unit 5's dribbling. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: five passes in a row in a pair, and a dribble-and-send at a target; knowledge task: says why one cue helps a throw or a pass; conduct: passes to a partner's chest, never at a body, and fetches on the signal, S3.5.4 review), 1 respond day. Tools: soft balls, foam kicking balls, hoops and cones | Passing on the move needs the throw, catch and dribble of grade 3 and must come before unit 11's attacking play, which is built on passing to open players | Candidate: a pass-and-move pattern a pair invents and shows | Targets are a goal, hoop or wall, never a person; one sending direction per row of pairs. Cost: $0 |
| 11 | 4 | Attack together. *How does my team move the ball into the other team's area, and how do we decide who does what so everyone is part of it?* | 11 | g4-attack-ideas; g4-team-roles; g4-with-others | SHAPE.S2.5.2; SHAPE.S3.5.1; SHAPE.S3.5.2; SHAPE.S4.5.2; SHAPE.S4.5.5 | Cues: "spread out, pass to open, move to space"; the tactics-question; team talk. Settings: three-on-three keep-away and end-zone games with no elimination, on several small courts | Explain how to attack where teams move into the other team's area: spread out, pass to open players, move to space (S2.5.2); explain how others see a situation, a player who is left out or a slower player (S3.5.1), and talk with others to decide who does what (S3.5.2); describe how favorite activities connect a child with others (S4.5.2) and spot difficulties a group faces when moving together, too little space, uneven skill, one player taking every turn, with a fix (S4.5.5). Calm steps return when a game gets heated (review). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 1's safe-movement tactics and unit 5's kindness in groups. Out: defending (grade 5). Ends: 2 check days (skill task: moves to open space and receives a pass in a three-on-three game, unit 10's pass cue watched at the unit's first skill day and again here; knowledge tasks: names two attacking moves in a game picture; one connection and one group difficulty with a fix; conduct: takes a role and decides with the group using team talk, S3.5.2), 1 respond day | After unit 10 because attacking is passing to open players; team roles and group difficulties are taught here because small-sided team play is where they first arise; it sits before the mid-year check so the check samples standards 3 and 4 | Candidate: a group plan card where the team writes who does what before a game | Teams made privately by the teacher; a more and a less competitive court offered; no scores posted. Pinnies reused. Cost: $0 |
| 12 | 4 | Volley, bump and serve. *How do I keep a light ball in the air with my hands, and how do I serve it into open space?* | 10 | g4-hands-strike-levels; g4-serve | SHAPE.S1.5.13; SHAPE.S1.5.14; SHAPE.S1.5.15 | Cues: "get under it, hands together, push up"; "step, swing low, contact". Settings: alone, against a wall, with a partner; a serve over a low net or a rope line | Hit with the hands above waist level, an overhead volley or set (S1.5.13; overhead volleying starts in grade 4, from the tool), and below waist level, an underhand bump or strike (S1.5.14), with a light ball alone, to a wall and with a partner; serve from a fixed spot, underhand with a hand or paddle, into an open area over a low net or line (S1.5.15). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 2's catch and unit 6's paddle strike. Out: rallies in a net game (grade 5). Ends: 1 check day (skill task: three volleys in a row with a partner, with three of five serves into the target area as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: shows the hands for a volley and for a bump and says which goes with which ball height; conduct: retrieves balls on the signal and keeps the court lines, S3.5.4 review), 1 respond day | Volleying needs grade 3's catching and paddle contact; it closes the first half so the net-and-wall games of grade 5 have both skills | Candidate: a wall-volley count the child tries to beat against their own first try | Light trainer balls for wrists; a low net or a rope between two chairs; one serving direction. Safety read (flying objects, net set-up). Cost: $75 (15 light trainer balls, a low net or rope line $15) |
