1 | 6 | 13 | g6-analyze-skill; g6-throw-roll-form; g6-rules-safe | SHAPE.S2.8.18; SHAPE.S1.8.6; SHAPE.S3.8.6; SHAPE.S3.8.7 | Break a skill into preparation, action and follow through, watch a partner and name the part to fix and its cue (S2.8.18; skill-analysis introduced on the first skill days); roll, and throw underhand, sidearm and overhand, with good form in drills and in two modified target games (S1.8.6, in full: all four actions in drills and in games); follow game rules in the drill, the target game and the roll game (S3.8.6) and name and practice safe behavior during activity and with equipment: one throwing direction, equipment checked, water in warm weather (S3.8.7). Opens with retrieval tasks on grade 5's striking and fielding throws and fair decisions, and readiness checks on g4-object-ideas (key parts of object skills) and g3-stay-safe-own (staying safe on one's own) in the last ten minutes of day 1. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: two throwing forms, three of five on target, the baseline cue "step opposite foot"; knowledge task: name a part to fix and its cue for a pictured throw; conduct: follows the target game's rules and equipment steps in two settings), 1 respond day. Tools: soft throwing balls, cones, paper targets, cue and analysis cards (Curio Class writes them); an optional skill clip on the teacher's device | First because skill analysis is the band's new model and every later motor row uses it, and the class rules and safe behavior must be set before any game; throwing is the grade 6 skill closest to grade 5's work, so analysis is taught on a known pattern
2 | 6 | 11 | g6-fitness-parts; g6-stretch-types; g6-skill-fitness-technique | SHAPE.S2.8.9; SHAPE.S2.8.14; SHAPE.S1.8.4 | Name the health-related parts (endurance, strength, flexibility) and the skill-related parts (agility, balance, coordination, power, reaction time, speed) and tell them apart in a sort and one drill per part (S2.8.9); use dynamic stretches in warm-ups and static stretches in cool-downs and in training, building a warm-up and a cool-down (S2.8.14, in full: warm-ups, cool-downs and training); sound technique in skill-related fitness exercises: a tape agility ladder, a hop and balance sequence, a partner reaction drill and a power jump with no equipment (S1.8.4). Health's physical activity outcome 3 (warm-up and cool-down) is taught here. Opens with a retrieval task on grade 5's FITT and pulse-check (a pulse after the warm-up). Ends: 1 check day (skill task: one skill-related drill with its cue, the power jump's soft landing watched at baseline; knowledge task: sort ten activities and build a three-move warm-up and a cool-down; conduct: rules and safe spacing at stations, S3.8.7 review), 1 respond day. Tools: tape ladders, activity sort cards (ours) | Second because every later unit opens with the warm-up and closes with the cool-down this unit teaches, and units 3 and 10 build on the fitness parts
3 | 6 | 12 | g6-heart-rate; g6-health-fitness-technique | SHAPE.S2.8.15; SHAPE.S1.8.3 | Understand heart rate and what it shows about aerobic fitness: resting and working rates, beats per minute from a ten- or fifteen-second count, and why the rate for the same jog drops over weeks of training (S2.8.15); sound technique in health-building fitness activities: a body-weight squat, a push-up on knees or toes, a plank, a jog with posture and a held stretch after the warm-up (S1.8.3, in full: a variety, one from each health-related part). The count times four or six is multiplication from earlier grades; ratio words come from math grade 6 unit 2 (days 19 to 39), taught first (Cross-subject). Opens with a readiness check on g4-pulse-body in the last ten minutes of unit 2's last day, and a retrieval task on unit 2's fitness parts. Water during activity (health's physical activity outcome 4) is taught on the jog days. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: one exercise from each health-related part with its cue, the squat's "neutral spine" watched at baseline; knowledge task: count, convert to beats per minute and say what a lower working rate after training shows; conduct: takes a private count and keeps others' counts private, S3.8.7 review), 1 respond day. Out: target heart-rate zones as a training method (grade 9) | After unit 2 because the strength exercises are health-related parts named there and the warm-up is in place; after math's ratio unit, so beats per minute rests on taught ratio language
4 | 6 | 12 | g6-catch-form; g6-pass-receive; g6-welcome-differences | SHAPE.S1.8.8; SHAPE.S1.8.14; SHAPE.S3.8.1; SHAPE.S3.8.2 | Catch with good form, with and without gear (a glove or a scoop), in drills and small-sided games (S1.8.8, in full); pass and receive while moving in small-sided games (S1.8.14); understand and welcome differences among classmates (ability, size, background, language) (S3.8.1) and show care and contribute helpfully to a team (S3.8.2). tactics-question first used here (where is the space, and what is my best move?). Opens with retrieval tasks on grade 5's invasion and fielding skills and fair decisions, and on grade 5's conflict solving for the team-talk days. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: catches a tossed ball at three heights, the baseline cue "give"; five passes on the move in a three-on-three drill, as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: name two ways a teammate can help a classmate who is new to the skill; conduct: a helpful contribution and an inclusive pass in the keep-away), 1 respond day. Tools: soft and playground balls, milk-jug scoops (low-equipment) or gloves, pinnies | Catching and passing need unit 1's skill analysis and throwing form; welcoming differences comes with the first team game of the year, where it first matters
5 | 6 | 14 | g6-dribble; g6-defensive-ready; g6-movement-ideas | SHAPE.S1.8.15; SHAPE.S1.8.19; SHAPE.S2.8.1 | Use a dribbling skill (hands and feet) in drills and small-sided games: control while traveling, change of direction, head up (S1.8.15); get into a ready defensive position and shuffle in drills and small games (S1.8.19); name how to use the movement ideas space, time, force and balance well when the setting keeps changing, in an obstacle course, a tag game and a small-sided game (S2.8.1, in full: all three settings). Opens with retrieval tasks on grade 5's defending ideas and problem solving, and on unit 4's passing. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: dribble through cones and past a passive defender, the baseline cue "head up"; the defensive stance and shuffle as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: name the movement idea used to solve a pictured problem in each of two settings; conduct: plays the no-one-out tag rule and gives a teammate space, S3.8.6 review), 1 respond day | After the mid-year check, so dribbling and defense rest on unit 4's passing; movement ideas sit here because the changing settings are the dribble and defend games
6 | 6 | 14 | g6-dance-sequences; g6-express-variety | SHAPE.S1.8.2; SHAPE.S4.8.1; SHAPE.S4.8.3 | Movement sequences in a range of dance styles: eight-count phrases, transitions and a sequence of four phrases (S1.8.2). Picks: Tinikling, a Philippine folk dance from Leyte where dancers step between two bamboo poles (tape lines first, then poles on the floor, then slowly moving poles; no "national dance" claim, as the claim is not settled at source); the Virginia Reel, a US country dance in longways sets, with teacher-set partners and no gender roles; a four-wall line dance in eight counts of our own, with no cultural claim; and a group's own rhythmic routine. Explain how self-expression affects how involved a person gets (S4.8.1), and take part in many kinds of activity that improve health, recording the kinds taken part in this year (units 2 and 3 fitness, units 4 and 5 team games, this unit's dances) and saying which fits best (S4.8.3, in full); goal-cycle and calm-steps first used here (a goal for the routine; a calm step before performing for a small group). Opens with a retrieval task on grade 5's gym and dance sequence and review of experiences. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: a four-phrase sequence to the beat, the baseline cue "transition"; knowledge task: one way self-expression changed how a person joined in, and the kind of activity that fits best and why; conduct: welcomes a partner's ideas in the group routine, S3.8.1 review), 1 respond day. Tools: music on the teacher's device or the teacher's count; PVC poles or ropes; dance cards and each dance's source line (Curio Class writes them) | Dance needs a year of movement ideas (unit 5) for levels and transitions; S4 sits here because a performance is where self-expression shows, and the record looks back on the year's kinds of activity
7 | 6 | 14 | g6-strike-net-toward; g6-ways-active | SHAPE.S2.8.16 | Work toward the striking and net skills grade 7 owns: a handheld forehand, a swing at a tossed ball and an underhand serve with a short tool, in modified rallies over a low net with light equipment, each begun toward its owner: SHAPE.S1.8.5 (grade 7, unit 11), SHAPE.S1.8.7 (grade 7, unit 11), SHAPE.S1.8.10 and SHAPE.S1.8.11 (grade 7, unit 12), SHAPE.S1.8.12 (grade 7, unit 12). List ways to stay active (team sports, individual sports, dance, outdoor, community programs, active transport), with ones that fit different interests, budgets and abilities, and how to find each (S2.8.16). Opens with retrieval tasks on grade 5's net and wall and striking skills and goals, and a readiness check on g4-reasons-strengths (reasons to move) at the end of unit 6. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: contact a tossed ball with a swing, the baseline cue "eyes on contact"; knowledge task: a personal list of five ways to stay active with how to find each; conduct: one swinger per space and courtesy in the rally, S3.8.7 review), 1 respond day. Tools: short paddles, foam bats, light balls, a rope net; community activity cards (invented for practice, no program or company names) | Last in grade 6: the striking tries hand straight into grade 7's striking units, and ways to stay active closes the year looking outward; it ends before the year-end check, which samples it
8 | 7 | 12 | g7-throw-power; g7-game-plans-feedback | SHAPE.S1.8.9; SHAPE.S3.8.3; SHAPE.S3.8.4 | Throw with accuracy, distance and power in drills and small-sided games: to a target, a long throw, a throw to a base (S1.8.9, in full); talk over a team's game plan before and between rounds (S3.8.3); give and take one kind, specific comment tied to a cue in a teacher-set peer-watching task (S3.8.4). Catching the partner's throw keeps g6-catch-form in use (review). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 1's throwing form and skill analysis and unit 4's welcoming differences. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: one throw each way, the baseline cue "hips, then shoulders"; knowledge task: which cue gives the power, and a team plan for a pictured game; conduct: gives and takes a specific comment in the peer task), 1 respond day. Tools: soft balls, bases, feedback cards with sentence frames (ours) | First in grade 7: it reopens the year on unit 1's throw through retrieval and adds power; feedback is taught on a skill the pairs already know, before the team games of units 9 and 11
9 | 7 | 12 | g7-invasion-attack; g7-invasion-defend; g7-net-invasion-toward | SHAPE.S2.8.2; SHAPE.S2.8.3 | Explain attacking moves that create space: spread out, move without the ball, make a cut, pass and move (S2.8.2); explain defending moves that close space and block opponents: person-to-person and zone, closing down, staying goal-side (S2.8.3); first watched tries at the skills grade 8 owns, a volley against a wall, a stick dribble, a shot at a goal and a cut to get open, each begun toward its owner: SHAPE.S1.8.13 (grade 8, unit 17), SHAPE.S1.8.16, SHAPE.S1.8.17 and SHAPE.S1.8.18 (grade 8, unit 16). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 4's passing and unit 5's dribble, stance and movement ideas. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: a cut to get open, then a pass, in a three-on-two drill, the baseline cue "move without the ball"; knowledge tasks: name two attacking moves and point to the space on a diagram; pick a defense for a diagram and say why; conduct: plays a fair game with a team plan, S3.8.3 review), 1 respond day | After unit 8's game plans; attack and defend use grade 6's passing, dribbling and stance, and the first tries prepare grade 8
10 | 7 | 12 | g7-fitt-principles; g7-skill-fitness-use; g7-effort-aerobic | SHAPE.S2.8.11; SHAPE.S2.8.12; SHAPE.S2.8.13 | Use FITT, progression, overload and specificity across different activities: raise one part of FITT at a time and match the plan to the goal (S2.8.11, in full: all four across at least two activities); use what is known about skill-related fitness in different activities: which parts matter most in a sport and a drill for each (S2.8.12); describe how aerobic fitness relates to how hard activity feels, with the one-to-ten effort scale and the talk test, and why the same pace feels easier over weeks (S2.8.13). The rate of effort or pace against time leans on math grade 6's ratio work the year before (Cross-subject). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 2's fitness parts and stretch types and unit 3's heart rate. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: keep a chosen pace at "talk test" effort for the time set, the baseline cue "steady breath"; knowledge tasks: change a plan for a different goal and say why; two skill-related parts for a sport with a drill each; conduct: private records kept private, S3.8.7 review), 1 respond day | After the grade 6 fitness units it builds on; before the grade 8 personal fitness test, which needs these principles
11 | 7 | 12 | g7-long-tool-swing; g7-strike-tossed; g7-settle-disagreements | SHAPE.S1.8.5; SHAPE.S1.8.7; SHAPE.S3.8.8; SHAPE.S3.8.9 | Swing a long-handled tool to hit with a mature pattern, with a bat, a hockey-style stick or a golf-style club on a tee or a tossed ball (S1.8.5); strike a self-dropped or tossed ball with a tool into open space, choosing a place to hit (S1.8.7); settle disagreements with teammates and with opponents using calm steps and team talk (S3.8.8) and use and respect the norms of good conduct: honest calls, a handshake or fist bump (S3.8.9). Fielding the hit keeps g6-catch-form in use (review). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 7's first swings and grade 6's rules, and a readiness check on g5-conflict-solve at the end of unit 10. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: contact a tossed ball with a mature pattern, three of five, the baseline cue "rotate"; a hit placed to a named zone as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: the steps to settle a pictured disagreement; conduct: settles a real call and keeps the norms in the self-officiated game), 1 respond day. Tools: foam bats, tees (cones), soft balls | After unit 7's first swings and unit 8's team plans; settling disagreements fits a self-officiated game, the first of the year
12 | 7 | 14 | g7-serves; g7-strokes; g7-manage-feelings | SHAPE.S1.8.10; SHAPE.S1.8.11; SHAPE.S1.8.12; SHAPE.S4.8.8 | Serve with the hand, underhand and overhand (S1.8.10, in full), and with a short or long tool, underhand and overhand (S1.8.11, in full), in drills and modified net and wall games; forehand and backhand strokes with a short or long tool (S1.8.12, in full: both strokes, both tool lengths across the stations); use several strategies to manage feelings and behavior during activity: slow breathing, self-talk, a short reset, asking for a role change (S4.8.8; calm-steps in full, in SEL's grade 6 words). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 7's first serves and strokes and grade 6's self-expression, and a readiness check on g5-review-experiences in the last ten minutes of the mid-year respond day. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: five serves into the court, to a named zone, the baseline cue "toss, step"; a three-stroke rally as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: name three strategies and when to use each; conduct: uses one strategy after a lost point, watched, S3.8.9 review), 1 respond day. Tools: paddles, light balls, beach or trainer balls for hand serves, rope nets | After the mid-year check: serves and strokes need unit 11's swing control; net games bring the most lost points, so managing feelings is taught where frustration is real
13 | 7 | 13 | g7-dance-ideas; g7-people-interests; g7-challenges | SHAPE.S2.8.8; SHAPE.S4.8.2; SHAPE.S4.8.4; SHAPE.S4.8.5 | Use movement ideas (space, time, force, relationship) to change different dance and rhythm forms: a unison section, a canon, a contrast, in a line dance, a circle dance and a rhythm form (S2.8.8). Pick: kolo, a Serbian chain dance danced in a circle at community gatherings (on UNESCO's intangible heritage list since 2017), taught in a simple step pattern, then changed with canon and contrast. Explain how time with others affects involvement (S4.8.2) and link choice and interests to how engaged a person becomes (S4.8.4): groups choose their dance style and roles; closely examine challenges faced alone and as a group in movement (fear of failing, space, a fitness gap) and test one fix in rehearsal (S4.8.5). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 6's sequences and self-expression and unit 5's movement ideas. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: perform a sequence changed with one idea, the baseline cue "canon"; knowledge tasks: say which idea changed it; connect a choice to staying involved; name a challenge with a fix and say how the fix went; conduct: includes every group member's idea, S3.8.2 review), 1 respond day. Tools: music on the teacher's device or claps; rhythm sticks (rolled paper on the low-equipment path) | Dance returns each year (units 6, 13, 18, 22) so it is never taught once and dropped; involvement and challenges sit with group choreography, where choice and others shape joining in
14 | 7 | 15 | g7-fitness-dance-outdoor-use; g7-rest; g7-eat-move-well | SHAPE.S2.8.17; SHAPE.S2.8.20 | Fitness, dance and an outdoor walk used as settings for form: form kept in a six-station circuit, a routine and a walk with a map and pace, used in new settings for SHAPE.S1.8.2 (grade 6, unit 6), SHAPE.S1.8.3 (grade 6, unit 3) and SHAPE.S1.8.4 (grade 6, unit 2), and begun toward SHAPE.S1.8.1 (grade 8, unit 20). Analyze how rest changes how the body responds to exercise: recovery days, sleep and the signs of too much (S2.8.17); describe how eating, exercise and well-being fit together: fuel before and after, water, sleep and mood (S2.8.20). Science grade 7 unit 3 (days 42 to 66) teaches how body systems work together and use food for energy first; PE uses the idea. Health's physical activity outcome 4 (water during activity) returns here. The outdoor rules keep g6-rules-safe in use (review). Opens with retrieval tasks on the grade 6 fitness and dance units and unit 7's ways to stay active, and a readiness check on g5-food-drink at the end of unit 13. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: form kept in the circuit of six stations, the baseline cue "posture"; knowledge tasks: plan rest days in a week of activity; describe how fuel, water and sleep help a pictured week; conduct: stays with the group and follows the route rules, S3.8.7 review), 1 respond day. About 6 minutes of each outdoor day go to set-up, the head count and the return. Tools: printed school-grounds maps (Curio Class makes a template the teacher fills in), circuit cards | Last in grade 7: spring weather suits the walk; it follows science's body systems unit, and the rest and fuel rows look back on the year's training (unit 10)
15 | 8 | 12 | g8-fitness-test-track; g8-tech-judge; g8-goals-obstacles | SHAPE.S2.8.10; SHAPE.S2.8.19; SHAPE.S4.8.6; SHAPE.S4.8.7 | Choose a personal fitness test and use it to track progress toward a goal over a term, privately and by choice: a baseline, a goal, a plan built with unit 10's principles, a retest and a plan change if progress stalls (S2.8.10, in full); judge how useful technology is for reaching activity and fitness goals: accuracy, privacy, cost and what a tool cannot tell, on printed tool cards invented for practice (S2.8.19); set participation goals from an honest look at one's own abilities (S4.8.6) and weigh the chances and obstacles for being active outside class: time, cost, transport, weather (S4.8.7). The retest uses a plot of the student's own results over weeks, leaning on math grade 6 unit 8 (days 142 to 157) two years before. Days: 11 in weeks 1 to 4, plus one held day: the retest and plan change, with the unit's end-of-unit knowledge task, held to course day 47 (week 16, inside unit 18's span), so the plan runs a term in every warm-up between; the scope and sequence sets it in days.csv. Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 10's training principles and effort and unit 13's challenges. Ends: 1 check day (the held day; skill task: the chosen test done with form, the baseline cue "steady pace"; knowledge tasks: shows the teacher the plan change and its reason, not the numbers; rates a tool card on three points; a participation goal, an obstacle and a plan around it; conduct: keeps others' results private, S3.8.7 review), 1 respond day | First in grade 8 so a goal can be tracked over a term; it builds on grade 7's training principles and challenges, and its goal and obstacle rows give the year its S4 thread
16 | 8 | 15 | g8-tool-dribble; g8-shoot; g8-get-open; g8-invasion-play | SHAPE.S1.8.16; SHAPE.S1.8.17; SHAPE.S1.8.18 | Dribble an object with a tool (a floor-hockey or lacrosse-style stick) in drills and small games (S1.8.16); shoot at a goal, with and without a tool (S1.8.17, in full); use a few different moves to get open while attacking: a cut, a fake, a change of pace (S1.8.18). Passing, dribbling and the defensive stance used in a modified four-on-four invasion game, full court only where space allows: used in new settings, SHAPE.S1.8.14 (grade 6, unit 4), SHAPE.S1.8.15 and SHAPE.S1.8.19 (grade 6, unit 5). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 9's first tries and attacking moves and unit 8's throw for power. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: dribble around cones and past a passive defender, the baseline cue "stick low"; shots from three spots as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: name the move used to get open in a pictured play; conduct: fair play and the stick rule in the game, S3.8.9 review), 1 respond day. Tools: floor-hockey sticks (pool noodles on the low-equipment path), soft pucks or balls, cone goals, pinnies | After the fitness unit; it owns the grade 8 invasion skills grade 7 began (unit 9) and combines the band's invasion skills before the mid-year check samples them
17 | 8 | 13 | g8-volley; g8-net-wall-play; g8-net-wall-tactics; g8-problem-methods | SHAPE.S1.8.13; SHAPE.S2.8.4; SHAPE.S2.8.7 | Volley with a short or long tool in drills and modified net and wall games (S1.8.13, in full: both tool lengths, both net and wall); serves and strokes used in a modified net or wall game, singles and doubles with courtesy calls: used in new settings, SHAPE.S1.8.10, SHAPE.S1.8.11 and SHAPE.S1.8.12 (grade 7, unit 12); pick and use the right shot and technique, placing away from the opponent and choosing short or deep (S2.8.4); use problem-solving methods in many games and activities: name the problem, try a change, test it, keep or drop it (S2.8.7; also used on unit 16's game). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 12's serves and strokes, unit 9's attacking moves and unit 8's game plans. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: a rally of three volleys with a partner, the baseline cue "contact in front"; a rally with a serve as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge tasks: choose a shot for a court picture and say why; record a change and its result; conduct: courtesy calls in doubles, S3.8.9 review), 1 respond day. Tools: paddles, light balls, rope nets | After unit 16 so the problem-solving method can be tested on two kinds of game; it owns the volley grade 7 began
18 | 8 | 8 | g8-fitness-dance-apply; g8-leading | SHAPE.S3.8.10 | Dance routines and fitness circuits used to show form in a planned week, with a self-chosen routine: used in new settings, SHAPE.S1.8.2 (grade 6, unit 6), SHAPE.S1.8.3 (grade 6, unit 3) and SHAPE.S1.8.4 (grade 6, unit 2). Describe how communication, feedback, teamwork and courtesy connect to leading, and lead a small-group warm-up or drill (S3.8.10). Holds unit 15's held retest day (course day 47), so units 16 and 17 start one day earlier than the even run and this unit's own 8 days are course days 40 to 46 and 48; the calendar file shows the even run (unit 15 on days 1 to 12), and the scope sets the exact days. Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 14's circuit and routine and unit 8's feedback and unit 11's conduct norms. Ends: 1 check day (skill task: perform the routine or circuit with correct form, the baseline cue "posture"; knowledge task: link two of communication, feedback, teamwork and courtesy to a pictured leader's choice; conduct: leads a three-minute warm-up for a small group), 1 respond day | Just before the mid-year check, so the leadership is the year's S3 owned code sampled there; short because its motor row reuses owned grade 6 codes
19 | 8 | 12 | g8-striking-fielding-play; g8-strike-field-tactics | SHAPE.S2.8.5; SHAPE.S2.8.6 | Striking, throwing and catching used in a modified striking and fielding game with no elimination: batting, fielding, throwing to a base: used in new settings, SHAPE.S1.8.5 and SHAPE.S1.8.7 (grade 7, unit 11), SHAPE.S1.8.6 and SHAPE.S1.8.8 (grade 6, units 1 and 4) and SHAPE.S1.8.9 (grade 7, unit 8). Explain attacking tactics in striking and fielding games, hit to space and run on contact (S2.8.5), and where to place defenders to cover space (S2.8.6). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 11's swing and placement, unit 8's throw and grade 6's catching (kept in use in units 8 and 11), and unit 9's attack and defense. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: a placed hit in the game, the baseline cue "rotate"; a field-and-throw to a base as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge tasks: place fielders on a diagram and say why; name an attacking tactic for a pictured runner; conduct: honest calls and a kind word after an out, S3.8.9 review), 1 respond day. Tools: foam bats, tees, soft balls, bases | After the mid-year check, in spring when fields are usable; it combines grade 7's swing and throw with grade 6's catch
